    "result_text": "The full build passes green. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I implemented\n\n`membershipTier` now becomes `GOLD` when the owner's household (owners sharing the same `householdId` \u2014 matched by normalized last name + normalized address, the same rule `RejectDuplicateHousehold`/`HouseholdId` use) has 3 or more members after this create; otherwise the existing SILVER/BRONZE rules apply. GOLD takes precedence.\n\nFollowing the codebase's existing `namesakeCount` pattern (a create-time-computed, persisted input that the mapper reads back), I:\n\n1. **`CountHousehold.java`** (new function) \u2014 mirrors `CountNamesakes`: counts existing household members via `ownerRepository.findAll()` (which sees only pre-existing owners, as it runs before save) plus this owner, and sets `householdSize`.\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `countHousehold` into the pipeline after `countNamesakes`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the persisted `householdSize` field (`household_size` column) with getter/setter.\n4. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 extended the `membershipTier` expression: `GOLD` when `householdSize >= 3`, else the prior SILVER/BRONZE logic.\n5. **Schema files** (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `household_size` column; fixed the hsqldb `data.sql` positional seed INSERTs by adding the trailing value (the other DBs use explicit column lists, so were unaffected).\n6. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the field's description to document the GOLD rule.\n\n## Test result\n\n`AcceptanceTest.coreGoldForThreeMemberHousehold` passes, and the full `./mvnw test` run is **BUILD SUCCESS** (1 test, 0 failures, 0 errors). The three household members share last name + address with `sharesHousehold=true`, so the third computes `householdSize=3` \u2192 `GOLD`, overriding the BRONZE it would otherwise get (no email, non-zero namesake count).",
